  "result": "Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05378)\nciteturn7283academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding ... It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels).\nTitle: Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\nAuthors: Andreas Motzfeldt, Joakim Edin, Casper L. Christensen, Christian Hardmeier, Lars Maaløe, Anna Rogers\nDate: Thu Sep  4 16:31:38 2025\n\nIn medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce Code Like Humans: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes (fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited). Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its `blind spots' (codes that are systematically undercoded).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04537)\nciteturn7283academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; We investigate the emergent social dynamics of Large Language Model (LLM) agents in a spatially extended El Farol Bar problem, observing how they autonomously navigate this classic social dilemma.\nTitle: Emergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem\nAuthors: Ryosuke Takata, Atsushi Masumori, Takashi Ikegammi\nDate: Thu Sep  4 08:09:42 2025\n\nWe investigate the emergent social dynamics of Large Language Model (LLM) agents in a spatially extended El Farol Bar problem, observing how they autonomously navigate this classic social dilemma. As a result, the LLM agents generated a spontaneous motivation to go to the bar and changed their decision making by becoming a collective. We also observed that the LLM agents did not solve the problem completely, but rather behaved more like humans. These findings reveal a complex interplay between external incentives (prompt-specified constraints such as the 60\\% threshold) and internal incentives (culturally-encoded social preferences derived from pre-training), demonstrating that LLM agents naturally balance formal game-theoretic rationality with social motivations that characterize human behavior. These findings suggest that a new model of group decision making, which could not be handled in the previous game-theoretic problem setting, can be realized by LLM agents.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05360)\nciteturn7283academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection ... In this work, we propose a novel approach inspired by ROUGE that constructs an N-Gram frequency tensor from LLM-generated text.\nTitle: Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection\nAuthors: Jerry Li, Evangelos Papalexakis\nDate: Wed Sep  3 18:52:24 2025\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated effectiveness across a wide variety of tasks involving natural language, however, a fundamental problem of hallucinations still plagues these models, limiting their trustworthiness in generating consistent, truthful information. Detecting hallucinations has quickly become an important topic, with various methods such as uncertainty estimation, LLM Judges, retrieval augmented generation (RAG), and consistency checks showing promise. Many of these methods build upon foundational metrics, such as ROUGE, BERTScore, or Perplexity, which often lack the semantic depth necessary to detect hallucinations effectively. In this work, we propose a novel approach inspired by ROUGE that constructs an N-Gram frequency tensor from LLM-generated text. This tensor captures richer semantic structure by encoding co-occurrence patterns, enabling better differentiation between factual and hallucinated content. We demonstrate this by applying tensor decomposition methods to extract singular values from each mode and use these as input features to train a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) binary classifier for hallucinations. Our method is evaluated on the HaluEval dataset and demonstrates significant improvements over traditional baselines, as well as competitive performance against state-of-the-art LLM judges.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantized Large Language Models in Biomedical Natural Language Processing: Evaluation and Recommendation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04534)\nciteturn7283academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: Quantized Large Language Models in Biomedical Natural Language Processing: Evaluation and Recommendation ... These findings provide significant practical and guiding value, highlighting quantization as a practical and effective strategy for enabling the secure, local deployment of large yet high-capacity language models in biomedical contexts, bridging the gap between technical advances in AI and real-world clinical translation.\nTitle: Quantized Large Language Models in Biomedical Natural Language Processing: Evaluation and Recommendation\nAuthors: Zaifu Zhan, Shuang Zhou, Min Zeng, Kai Yu, Meijia Song, Xiaoyi Chen, Jun Wang, Yu Hou, Rui Zhang\nDate: Thu Sep  4 04:18:45 2025\n\nLarge language models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in biomedical natural language processing, yet their rapid growth in size and computational requirements present a major barrier to adoption in healthcare settings where data privacy precludes cloud deployment and resources are limited. In this study, we systematically evaluated the impact of quantization on 12 state-of-the-art large language models, including both general-purpose and biomedical-specific models, across eight benchmark datasets covering four key tasks: named entity recognition, relation extraction, multi-label classification, and question answering. We show that quantization substantially reduces GPU memory requirements-by up to 75%-while preserving model performance across diverse tasks, enabling the deployment of 70B-parameter models on 40GB consumer-grade GPUs. In addition, domain-specific knowledge and responsiveness to advanced prompting methods are largely maintained. These findings provide significant practical and guiding value, highlighting quantization as a practical and effective strategy for enabling the secure, local deployment of large yet high-capacity language models in biomedical contexts, bridging the gap between technical advances in AI and real-world clinical translation.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem | Cognoska (https://cognoska.com/paper/emergent-social-dynamics-of-llm-agents-in-the-el-farol-bar-problem-9a8fbdb0-38b9-4210-aeb6-625184580e4a)\nciteturn7283search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; We investigate the emergent social dynamics of Large Language Model (LLM) agents in a spatially extended El Farol Bar problem, observing how they autonomously navigate this classic social dilemma.\n\n# Emergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem\n\nSep 4, 2025•\n\nRyosuke Takata, Atsushi Masumori, Takashi Ikegami\n\nScore: 0.87\n\ncs.MAArtificial IntelligenceComputers and Society\n\nAsk the paper a questionOpen in dashboardOpen original paper\n\nPaper PDF\n\nPDF preview is not supported reliably on mobile browsers.\n\nOpen PDF\n\nProblem\n\nInvestigating how Large Language Model (LLM) agents autonomously navigate and make decisions in a spatially extended El Farol Bar problem, focusing on their emergent social dynamics and decision-making processes.\n\nApproach\n\nUsing communicative LLM-based agents to simulate autonomous decision-making in the El Farol Bar problem within a two-dimensional space, where agents communicate with each other and adapt their behavior based on feedback from the environment and past interactions.\n\nResults\n\nThe LLM agents generated spontaneous motivation to go to the bar and changed their decision-making by becoming a collective. They did not solve the problem completely but behaved more like humans, balancing formal game-theoretic rationality with social motivations that characterize human behavior.\n\nSummary\n\nWe investigate the emergent social dynamics of Large Language Model (LLM) agents in a spatially extended El Farol Bar problem, observing how they autonomously navigate this classic social dilemma. As a result, the LLM agents generated a spontaneous motivation to go to the bar and changed their decision making by becoming a collective. We also observed that the LLM agents did not solve the problem completely, but rather behaved more like humans. These findings reveal a complex interplay between external incentives (prompt-specified constraints such as the 60% threshold) and internal incentives (culturally-encoded social preferences derived from pre-training), demonstrating that LLM agents naturally balance formal game-theoretic rationality with social motivations that characterize human behavior. These findings suggest that a new model of group decision making, which could not be handled in the previous game-theoretic problem setting, can be realized by LLM agents.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCode Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding (https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1231.pdf)\nciteturn7283search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding ... We introduce *Code Like Humans*: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models.It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels).\nCode Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\n\nAndreas Motzfeldt¹,² Joakim Edin¹,³ Casper L. Christensen¹  \nChristian Hardmeier² Lars Maaløe¹ Anna Rogers²\n\n¹Corti.ai ²IT University of Denmark ³University of Copenhagen\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce *Code Like Humans*: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes. Fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited. Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its ‘blind spots’ (codes that are systematically undercoded).\n\nCode: https://github.com/MotzWanted/codeseeker\n\n## 1 Introduction\nAlthough this resource is an authoritative source of information used by medical coders to narrow down the search space, the current approaches do not consider it in any way.\n\nTo address this, we propose *CLH* (Code Like Humans): an LLM-based agentic framework² that leverages ICD resources such as the alphabetic index. *CLH* is inspired by the medical coding workflow developed for healthcare professionals in the UK (NHS England, 2023; CMS and NCHS, 2025). Our approach is summarized in Figure 1.\n\n¹It takes a professional inpatient human coder approximately 30 minutes per case on average (Chen et al., 2021).  \n²An LLM-based agentic system expands the functionality of a stand-alone LLM in a goal-oriented manner to plan and take actions over time, interacting with tools, data sources, or other agents (Qiu et al., 2024).\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 1 overview diagram of the CLH framework with four labeled agents and example code outputs.  \n1. Evidence Extractor: “Extracts codable conditions from notes”; example note terms shown include “Sepsis”, “Esophageal adenocarcinoma”, “He quit smoking 15 years ago”, and “CVA without residual”.  \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNews | Ryosuke Takata (https://bonochof.github.io/news/)\nciteturn7283search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The research theme is \"Multi-Agent Language Emergence Based on Functional Requirements and Elucidation of Structuring Principles.\" ... The title is \"Emergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem.\" arXiv:2509.04537 ... The title is \"Analysis of the El Farol Bar Problem by LLM Agents.\"\n  * 2025.09.04 Preprint published. The title is \"Emergent Social Dynamics of LLM Agents in the El Farol Bar Problem.\" arXiv:2509.04537\n  * 2025.06.01 Co-authored paper is out. The title is \"Effects of Color Discrimination of Perceived Objects by Cognitive Speech Acts on the Reaction Time.\" The paper is here.\n  * 2025.05.29 Oral presentation at the 39th Annual Conference of the Japanese Society for Artificial Intelligence. The title is \"Analysis of the El Farol Bar Problem by LLM Agents.\"\n  * 2025.04.01 Appointed as a JSPS Research Fellow PD and Project Researcher at the Graduate School of Engineering, The University of Tokyo.\n  * 2025.03.24 Completed graduate school at the University of Tokyo and received a Doctor of Philosophy (Ph.D.) degree.\n  * 2025.03.01 Article published in the Journal of the Japanese Society for Artificial Intelligence. The title is \"JSAI Meets AI Standardization -- Panel Discussion Report: What Personnel Are Required for the Standardization Process? --\"\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Evangelos E. Papalexakis (https://dblp.org/pid/48/9024.html)\nciteturn7283search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection. ... Robust Vision-Language Models via Tensor Decomposition: A Defense Against Adversarial Attacks.\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2509-05360\n\nJerry Li, Evangelos E. Papalexakis:\nBeyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection. CoRR abs/2509.05360 (2025)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i58]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2509-16163\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection · Pith Review (https://pith.science/paper/2509.05360)\nciteturn7283search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; Applying LSA-style subspace features to n-grams for hallucination detection is a reasonable extension, and the tensor construction is clearly described. ... For group sizes > 1, the N-gram tensor—and hence the singular-value feature vector—for an evaluation-split group is a function of the true labels of the texts in that group. ... This inflates the apparent number of independent test examples and makes the comparison with per-text baselines (Perplexity, ROUGE-L, BERTScore, GPT-4o Judge) unfair.\n## Why Pith says this\n\nHighlight any text to comment on it.\n\nEditor's note read the letter\n\nFirst, the important thing: the paper's headline results are not real. The grouping step in Section 2.1 uses true labels to construct the features at evaluation time. For group sizes 5 and 20, each group contains only same-label texts, so the SVD features encode the answer being predicted. The per-text F1/accuracy in Table 2 are duplicated across group members with a single group-level decision. At inference on a single unseen text, you cannot build the features without already knowing the label. The stress-test note is exactly right.\n\nThat said, the paper has some merit as an idea. Applying LSA-style subspace features to n-grams for hallucination detection is a reasonable extension, and the tensor construction is clearly described. The authors also check for textual bias by trying binary and log-frequency matrices, which is a good instinct.\n\nThe problems are concentrated in the evaluation. The only non-circular configuration is G1, and there the method is at or near chance: General AUROC 0.54, Summary 0.54, Dialogue 0.68, all below or comparable to simple perplexity. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCode Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://arxiv.deeppaper.ai/papers/2509.05378v1?locale=en)\nciteturn7283search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding ... This paper introduces a multi-agent framework called \"Code Like Humans\" for medical coding using large language models, achieving state-of-the-art performance on rare diagnosis codes within the full ICD-10 system.\n\n# Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\n\nPDF DeepRead\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nThe key contribution is a novel multi-agent framework for medical coding that leverages large language models to achieve state-of-the-art performance on rare diagnosis codes within the full ICD-10 system, while also providing an analysis of system performance and identifying blind spots.\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn medical coding, experts map unstructured clinical notes to alphanumeric codes for diagnoses and procedures. We introduce Code Like Humans: a new agentic framework for medical coding with large language models. It implements official coding guidelines for human experts, and it is the first solution that can support the full ICD-10 coding system (+70K labels). It achieves the best performance to date on rare diagnosis codes (fine-tuned discriminative classifiers retain an advantage for high-frequency codes, to which they are limited). Towards future work, we also contribute an analysis of system performance and identify its `blind spots' (codes that are systematically undercoded).\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces a multi-agent framework called \"Code Like Humans\" for medical coding using large language models, achieving state-of-the-art performance on rare diagnosis codes within the full ICD-10 system.\n\n### Subjects\n\ncs.AI cs.MA\n\n### arXiv\n\nImage: arXiv 2509.05378v1\n\n### Paper Type\n\nmethod application framework\n\n### Domain\n\nAI ML Agent Medical Coding\n\nAuthors\n\nAndreas Motzfeldt, Joakim Edin, Casper L. Christensen, Christian Hardmeier, Lars Maaløe, Anna Rogers\n\nSubmitted\n\nSep 04, 2025--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCode Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/code-like-humans-a-multi-agent-solution-for-medical-coding/wrk_181736b10512105cad9360a319bc6ed6)\nciteturn7283search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding ... The authors hypothesize that this stagnation stems from a disconnect: AI models try to code purely from memory, whereas human coders rely heavily on external tools like alphabetical indexes and hierarchical coding guidelines. ... Extracted figure from page 1 of Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\n\n# Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\n\nAndreas Motzfeldt, Joakim Edin, Casper L. Christensen, Christian Hardmeier, Lars Maaløe, Anna Rogers\n\narXiv 2025\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThe paper introduces Code Like Humans (CLH), a multi-agent LLM framework that mimics the cognitive workflow of professional medical coders to map clinical notes to ICD-10 codes. It is the first AI solution capable of supporting the full ICD-10 solution space (+70K labels), achieving state-of-the-art performance on rare, long-tail diagnosis codes while matching traditional discriminative baselines that are restricted to small code subsets.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nMedical coding—the process of translating unstructured clinical narratives into standardized alphanumeric codes (like ICD-10)—is critical for healthcare statistics and hospital billing. However, it is an incredibly time-intensive task prone to human error.\n\nWhile NLP has attempted to automate this, progress has stagnated. \nImage: Overview of the Code-Like-Humans multi-agent frameworkExpand\n\nExtracted figure from page 1 of Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding\n\nThe framework distributes the task across four specialized agents:\n\n  1. Evidence Extractor: Processes the raw clinical note to extract verbatim text snippets representing codeable conditions.\n  2. Index Navigator: Takes these snippets and uses a vector-based semantic search index (Qdrant) to map them to official terms in the ICD alphabetical index, generating a preliminary list of candidate codes.\n  3. Tabular Validator: Evaluates the candidate codes in parallel against the tree-structured ICD hierarchy and chapter-specific official guidelines to resolve ambiguities and anatomical specificities.\n  4. Code Reconciler: Audits the tentative codes against instructional notes (e.g., mutually exclusive codes, ordering conventions) to produce the final, ordered list of medical codes.\n\nThe authors implemented this framework using \"thinking-enabled\" (reasoning) LLMs, experimenting with open-weight models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-70B, Qwen3-235B) and closed models (OpenAI o3-mini and o4-mini).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMind Meets Machines - OHDSI Symposium 2025 - Phenotype Development and Evaluation Work Group - General - OHDSI Forums (https://forums.ohdsi.org/t/mind-meets-machines-ohdsi-symposium-2025-phenotype-development-and-evaluation-work-group/24349)\nciteturn7283search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last year; Mount Sinai Health System (MSHS)  | Eyal Klang, MD (Corresponding Author): eyal.klang@mountsinai.org  | Evaluation of RAG-enhanced LLMs (GPT-4, Llama-3.1) for automating Emergency Department ICD-10-CM coding vs. human coders. ... CLH (Code Like Humans)  | Univ. of Cambridge/Microsoft Research Authors andreas@motzfeldt.dk ; Yu-Neng Chuang (Corresponding Author): yc577@cam.ac.uk  | LLM-based agentic framework designed to automate medical coding (ICD-10) by mirroring human processes.| Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding  |\n| Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding  | \nMedCodER  | Univ. of Illinois Urbana-Champaign Authors sanmitra1@gmail.com ; Y. Zhang & J. Gao (Corresponding Authors): {yuz9, jgao8}@illinois.edu  | Generative AI framework for automatic medical coding (ICD code prediction) using extraction, retrieval, and re-ranking.  | MedCodER: A Generative AI Assistant for Medical Coding  | \nErasmus MC - Medical Informatics  | Peter Rijnbeek, PhD (p.rijnbeek@erasmusmc.nl)  | Exploring NLP and LLMs for extracting concepts from clinical text (including non-English languages) for phenotyping.  | Erasmus MC - Medical Informatics  | \nThe Jackson Laboratory & Charité Berlin  | Peter N. Robinson, MD, MSc (Peter.Robinson@jax.org)  | Development of Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO); Computational deep phenotyping and ontology-based data models (Phenopackets).  | The Jackson Laboratory & Charité Berlin  | \nKOMAP  | University of Florida and NVIDIA Authors tcai@hsph.harvard.edu  | Knowledge-Driven Online Multimodal Automated Phenotyping; Uses knowledge graph embeddings on EHR concepts to generate feature lists.  | Knowledge-Driven Online Multimodal Automated Phenotyping System  | \nOpen-Source Tools & Consortia  |  |  |  | \nThe Monarch Initiative  | Melissa Haendel, PhD; Chris Mungall, PhD info@monarchinitiative.org  | International consortium for deep phenotyping; NLP/ML to extract phenotypic information and standardize using ontologies (HPO).  | Monarch Initiative  | \nLlettuce  | University of Manchester Authors g.figueredo@nottingham.ac.uk\nR.A.C. and G.D. (Corresponding Authors): {rebecca.croft, goran.davidovic}@manchester.ac.uk  | Open-source tool using local LLMs, semantic search, and fuzzy matching for converting medical terms into the OMOP standard vocabulary.  | Llettuce: An Open Source Natural Language Processing Tool for the Translation of Medical Terms into Uniform Clinical Encoding  | \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJerry Li - RankMe (https://rankdblp.com/authors/__name__jerry_li.html)\nciteturn7283search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; 2026  | J  | `jnl`  | AnimeScore: A Preference-Based Dataset and Framework for Evaluating Anime-Like Speech Style. ... 2025  | J  | `jnl`  | Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection. ... 2021  | J  | `jnl`  | Challenges of real-world reinforcement learning: definitions, benchmarks and analysis.\n2025  | J  | `jnl`  | BEVCALIB: LiDAR-Camera Calibration via Geometry-Guided Bird's-Eye View Representations. CoRR Weiduo Yuan, Jerry Li, Justin Yue, Divyank Shah, Konstantinos Karydis, Hang Qiu\n2025  | J  | `jnl`  | Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection. CoRR Jerry Li, Evangelos E. Papalexakis\n2025  | Misc  | `conf`  | Demo Abstract: C-Shenron: A Realistic Radar Simulation Framework for CARLA. SenSys Pushkal Mishra, Satyam Srivastava, Jerry Li, Kshitiz Bansal, Dinesh Bharadia\n2025  | Misc  | `conf`  | Demo Abstract: Cooperative Multi-modal Sensing. SenSys Bo Wu, Jerry Li, Ruoshen Mo, Justin Yue, Dinesh Bharadia, Hang Qiu\n2025  | J  | `jnl`  | Differential Multimodal Transformers. CoRR Jerry Li, Timothy Oh, Joseph Hoang, Vardhit Veeramachaneni\n2025  | J  | `jnl`  | High-Accuracy List-Decodable Mean Estimation. CoRR Ziyun Chen, Spencer Compton, Daniel Kane, Jerry Li\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCode Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding (https://aclanthology.org/anthology-files/anthology-files/pdf/findings/2025.findings-emnlp.1231.pdf)\nciteturn7283search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; document-level supervision into a multi-instance ... viding the entire ICD solution space as in-context ... manual for medical coding and provide instruc- ... Code Like Humans (CLH) Framework ... Human medical coders are healthcare profession- ... some similarity.6 The alphanumeric characters in a\nHence, the coder must verify that the candidate\ncode is the most specific code possible for this case\nin the ICD hierarchy.\nThe output of medical coding is not a single\ncode, but a list of applicable codes. The order of\nthose codes may itself be meaningful in some cases.\nThere are also rules for precedence between spe-\ncific codes, e.g., the code for “Alzheimer’s” should\ncome before the code for “dementia”.\nAll human coders must be familiar with official\nICD guidelines to ensure accurate and comprehen-\nsive coding. The current ICD-10-CM guidelines\nare 115 pages long. These guidelines serve as the\nmanual for medical coding and provide instruc-\ntions on using the alphabetical index and navigating\nthe hierarchy. Additionally, they provide chapter-\nspecific rules on selecting and combining codes,\nwhich are fundamental to clarifying otherwise am-\nbiguous choices among several candidate codes.\n4\nCode Like Humans (CLH) Framework\n4.1\nArchitecture\nHuman medical coders are healthcare profession-\nals with extensive training in one or more medical\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommunication, Embodiment, and Coordination in an El Farol Bar-Inspired LLM Society. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42636169/)\nciteturn7283search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last month; We study the social dynamics of Large Language Model (LLM) agents in a spatial environment with a bar, inspired by the El Farol Bar problem. ... More broadly, the study demonstrates how LLM-based agent societies can function as experimental systems for investigating emergent coordination and behavioral differentiation.\nIn contrast to the classical formulation, our model reintroduces local communication, embodied movement, and temporal delay: agents interact only with nearby others, must physically move to enter or leave the bar, and cannot immediately realize intended actions. Across 10 independent simulations, the system consistently regulated occupancy near the 60% crowding threshold, typically settling slightly above it. This collective pattern emerged not from explicit payoff optimization, but from local social interaction. Agents formed anticipatory clusters before overcrowding, produced context-sensitive communicative signals, and exhibited history-dependent divergence in their responses to congestion. In some runs, spontaneously generated hashtags temporarily inhibited departure, indicating that symbolic communication can stabilize short-term group cohesion. Together, these results suggest that coordination in an El Farol-inspired setting becomes socially mediated, temporally extended, and path-dependent once communication and embodiment are restored. More broadly, the study demonstrates how LLM-based agent societies can function as experimental systems for investigating emergent coordination and behavioral differentiation.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Large Language Models\n  * Communication\n  * Social Behavior\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * El Farol bar problem\n  * embodiment\n  * large language models\n  * multi-agent simulations\n  * social dynamics\n\n## Copyright\n\n© 2026 Massachusetts Institute of Technology.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nN-Gram Decoding (https://www.aussieai.com/research/n-gram-decoding)\nciteturn7283search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * Jerry Li, Evangelos Papalexakis, 3 Sep 2025, Beyond ROUGE: N-Gram Subspace Features for LLM Hallucination Detection, https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05360  * Mohsinul Kabir, Tasfia Tahsin, Sophia Ananiadou, 17 Sep 2025, From n-gram to Attention: How Model Architectures Learn and Propagate Bias in Language Modeling, https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12381\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Code Like Humans: A Multi-Agent Solution for Medical Coding (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395354698_Code_Like_Humans_A_Multi-Agent_Solution_for_Medical_Coding)\nciteturn7283search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Progress in NLP methods for medical coding ... rent modeling approaches and how human medical ... It takes a professional inpatient human coder approxi- ... Figure 1: Overview of Code-Like-Humans, our agentic ... arXiv:2509.05378v1 [cs.AI] 4 Sep 2025 ... This solution achieves comparable per- ... each agent. ... text using multi-ﬁlter residual convolutional neural ... Conclusion Large language 